scope: src/tools/scaffold
description: >
  Naming-enforcement tool that creates .aide files with correct filenames and applies the auto-rename rule when research enters a folder so agents cannot corrupt the spec layout.
intent: >
  aide_scaffold is the pen — the tool an agent calls to create new .aide
  files. Its reason for existing is not "write a file"; it is "enforce
  the naming conventions so an agent can never accidentally corrupt a
  folder's spec layout". The auto-rename rule (`.aide` becomes
  `intent.aide` the moment `research.aide` is introduced) is the rule
  agents are most likely to get wrong by hand, and this tool is where
  that rule is implemented once and trusted forever.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - type=intent in a folder with no research.aide creates `.aide`,
      the default form.
    - type=intent in a folder that already contains research.aide
      creates `intent.aide` instead, because the default `.aide` form
      is not allowed to coexist with a sibling research spec.
    - type=research creates `research.aide` and, if a `.aide` already
      exists in the folder, renames it to `intent.aide` in the same
      call — the default form cannot survive the introduction of
      research.
    - type=both creates both `intent.aide` and `research.aide`,
      applying the same rename-if-needed logic so the final state is
      always `intent.aide` + `research.aide`, never `.aide` +
      `research.aide`.
    - type=todo creates `todo.aide` unconditionally. todo specs are
      orthogonal to the intent/research pair and have no naming
      interaction with them.
    - type=plan creates `plan.aide` unconditionally. plan specs are
      orthogonal to the intent/research pair and have no naming
      interaction with them.
    - Re-running scaffold on a folder that already contains the
      requested spec reports "no changes needed" and leaves every file
      on disk untouched.
    - Every action the tool takes — create, rename, skip — is reported
      back as a plain-English line the agent can read and paste into a
      commit message.
  undesired:
    - "A scaffold call that creates `.aide` next to an existing
      `research.aide`. This is the single worst outcome: the folder
      now contains both `.aide` and `research.aide`, which violates
      the never-coexist rule, and a future validate run will flag it
      as a naming conflict. The tool was built to prevent exactly
      this, and must refuse to produce it."
    - A scaffold call that overwrites an existing spec. The caller
      may have meant to create; they did not mean to destroy. An
      existing file is a skip, not a target.
    - A scaffold call that produces `.aide` + `intent.aide` in the
      same folder. These are the same logical spec under two names;
      having both is corruption.
    - A scaffold call that renames `.aide` to `intent.aide` when no
      `research.aide` exists and is not being created. The rename is
      only correct as a response to research entering the folder. An
      unprompted rename breaks every reference that pointed at
      `.aide`.
    - A scaffold that returns a serialized status object instead of a
      line-per-action log. The agent reads the actions to confirm
      what happened; a JSON envelope buries that signal.
---

## Context

The AIDE naming rules exist so that an agent scanning a folder can
identify the spec layout from filenames alone. Two rules carry the
whole convention: `.aide` is the default single-spec form, and
`.aide` may never coexist with `research.aide` in the same folder —
whenever research enters a folder, the existing `.aide` must be
renamed to `intent.aide`. Humans can be trusted to follow this rule
when they have the full convention loaded in their head; agents
cannot, because every session starts cold. This tool is where the
rule is implemented once, so the rest of the system can trust that a
spec created through scaffold is a spec that obeys the convention.
`todo.aide` and `plan.aide` sit outside this rename machinery
entirely — they are orthogonal file types with fixed names that are
always safe to create without consulting the intent/research layout.

## Strategy

**The type parameter picks the desired end state, not the file
operations.** The caller says "I want an intent spec here" or "I want
both"; scaffold figures out which filenames and which renames are
required to reach that state from whatever the folder currently
contains. That separation is the whole reason the tool exists: the
caller should not have to reason about the `.aide`-vs-`intent.aide`
rule, and the tool is the single place that reasoning lives.

**Rename on research entry, never otherwise.** The only legal
transition from `.aide` to `intent.aide` is when `research.aide` is
being introduced to the same folder, because that is the only
situation in which the default form is no longer allowed. Any other
rename — speculative, cosmetic, "for consistency" — breaks references
and is wrong.

**Existing files are skipped, never overwritten.** A call that finds
the requested spec already in place reports "no changes needed" and
exits. Overwriting an existing file would be data loss, and the tool
has no way to tell whether the caller intended it. Safety wins.

**Actions are reported as prose, one per line.** The agent reads the
report to confirm what scaffold did. A bullet list of actions —
"Created .aide", "Renamed .aide → intent.aide", "Created research.aide"
— gives the agent exactly the signal it needs, in the form it can
paste into a commit message or cite back to the user. No serialized
envelope.

## Good examples

An agent calls `aide_scaffold` with `type=research` on a folder that
already contains `.aide`. The response is:

> Renamed .aide → intent.aide
> Created research.aide

Two actions, reported in order, and the folder is now in the only
legal `intent.aide` + `research.aide` configuration.

An agent calls `aide_scaffold` with `type=intent` on an empty folder.
The response is:

> Created .aide

Single action; the folder now contains the default single-spec form.

An agent calls `aide_scaffold` with `type=intent` on a folder that
already contains `research.aide`. The response is:

> Created intent.aide (research.aide exists, so using explicit name)

The caller asked for "intent"; the tool resolved the ambiguity using
the naming rule and told the caller exactly which filename it picked
and why.

An agent calls `aide_scaffold` with `type=plan` on any folder. The
response is:

> Created plan.aide

The folder's intent/research layout is untouched. `plan.aide` is
always created under its fixed name regardless of what other specs
are present.

An agent calls `aide_scaffold` a second time on a folder that is
already fully scaffolded. The response is:

> No changes needed — files already exist.

Nothing on disk changed. The call is safe to retry indefinitely.

## Bad examples

A scaffold call that writes `.aide` into a folder that already
contains `research.aide`, producing both. A validator will flag this
as a naming conflict, but the damage has already been done: the rule
that this tool exists to enforce has been broken by the tool itself.

A scaffold call that silently overwrites an existing `intent.aide`
when asked for `type=both`. The caller wanted to ensure both specs
exist; they did not ask to have their existing intent spec replaced
with a blank template. The operation is destructive and the caller
has no way to tell it happened until they open the file.

A scaffold call that returns:

> {"actions":[{"type":"create","file":".aide"},{"type":"create","file":"research.aide"}],"status":"ok"}

The agent now has to parse a schema to find out what happened. The
report was supposed to be readable; it is now data.
